## 2. Chip Thermal Model
**Files:** `Components/SubComponents/DataCenter/Blade/BaseClasses/chip_3D.mo`,
`CPU_3D.mo`, `GPU_3D.mo`, `Blade_coldplates.mo`

Both CPU and GPU use the same `chip_3D` base model. `CPU_3D` and `GPU_3D` each simply extend it:

```modelica
model CPU_3D
  extends Icons.CPU;
  extends chip_3D;
```

> **Source:** `CPU_3D.mo` — entire file. GPU_3D is identical with a different icon.

### 2.1 Geometry Parameters

| Parameter | Default Value | Source Line |
|-----------|--------------|-------------|
| nX, nY, nZ | 1, 1, 1 | `parameter Integer nX = 1 "No. of nodes in X"` |
| length_x | 0.5 m | `parameter Real length_x = 0.5 "Dimension in X in m"` |
| length_y | 0.2 m | `parameter Real length_y = 0.2 "Dimension in Y in m"` |
| length_z | 0.1 m | `parameter Real length_z = 0.1 "Dimension in Z in m"` |
| Material | Silicon | `redeclare package Material = ORNLSupercomputing.Components.SubComponents.Media.Silicon` |
| T_amb | 298.15 K | `parameter Modelica.Units.SI.Temperature Tamb = 298.15` |
| htc | 10 W/m²·K | `parameter Modelica.Units.SI.Temperature htc = 10.0 "Ambient HTC in W/m**2-K"` |

> **Source:** All from `chip_3D.mo` parameter declarations at the top of the file.
> Note: the `htc` parameter is mistyped as `SI.Temperature` in the original code but used as a heat transfer coefficient — a minor bug in the source.

### 2.2 Governing Equations

#### 3D Heat Conduction in Silicon

The primary physics engine is `TRANSFORM.HeatAndMassTransfer.DiscritizedModels.Conduction_3D`, which solves the transient heat equation over a 3D grid of nX × nY × nZ nodes:

```
ρ · cp · ∂T/∂t = k · (∂²T/∂x² + ∂²T/∂y² + ∂²T/∂z²) + Q_gen
```

> **Source:** `chip_3D.mo`:
> ```modelica
> TRANSFORM.HeatAndMassTransfer.DiscritizedModels.Conduction_3D conduction(
>   redeclare package Material = ORNLSupercomputing.Components.SubComponents.Media.Silicon,
>   redeclare model Geometry =
>       TRANSFORM.HeatAndMassTransfer.ClosureRelations.Geometry.Models.Plane_3D(
>       nX=nX, nY=nY, nZ=nZ,
>       length_x=length_x, length_y=length_y, length_z=length_z),
>   redeclare model InternalHeatModel = ...GenericHeatGeneration)
> ```
> The `Conduction_3D` component from TRANSFORM implements the 3D heat equation. The specific PDE form is defined inside the TRANSFORM library.

#### Convective Boundary Conditions (4 Exposed Side Faces)

Newton's law of cooling is applied to all four side faces using `TRANSFORM.HeatAndMassTransfer.Resistances.Heat.Convection`:

```
Q_conv = α · A · (T_surface - T_amb)
```

> **Source:** `chip_3D.mo` — four convection arrays declared for each face:
> ```modelica
> TRANSFORM.HeatAndMassTransfer.Resistances.Heat.Convection convectionX1[nY,nZ](
>     each alpha=htc, surfaceArea=conduction.geometry.crossAreas_1[1, :, :])
> TRANSFORM.HeatAndMassTransfer.Resistances.Heat.Convection convectionY2[nX,nZ](
>     each alpha=htc, surfaceArea=conduction.geometry.crossAreas_2[end, :, :])
> TRANSFORM.HeatAndMassTransfer.Resistances.Heat.Convection convectionX2[nY,nZ](
>     each alpha=htc, surfaceArea=conduction.geometry.crossAreas_1[end, :, :])
> TRANSFORM.HeatAndMassTransfer.Resistances.Heat.Convection convectionY1[nY,nZ](
>     each alpha=htc, surfaceArea=conduction.geometry.crossAreas_2[1, :, :])
> ```
> Each face connects to a fixed ambient temperature boundary `T_infX1`, `T_infX2`, `T_infY1`, `T_infY2` all set to `Tamb`.

#### Heat Input and Cold Plate Output

```modelica
connect(port_a, conduction.port_a3)   -- heat power input (W) from workload
connect(conduction.port_b3, port_b)   -- heat output to cold plate contact surface
```

> **Source:** `chip_3D.mo` equation section — the `port_a3` / `port_b3` connections in the `Conduction_3D` component represent the Z-direction faces (bottom and top of chip).

### 2.3 Blade Architecture

One blade (`Blade_coldplates.mo`) contains 2 GPUs and 2 CPUs in two parallel branches:

```
Branch 1: port_a → coldPlate(GPU) → coldPlate(CPU) → port_b
Branch 2: port_a → coldPlate1(GPU) → coldPlate3(CPU) → port_b
```

GPU heat is absorbed first — coolant enters cold plate at GPU temperature, exits warmer, then enters CPU cold plate. GPU thermal load directly raises the coolant temperature seen by the CPU.

> **Source:** `Blade_coldplates.mo` equation section:
> ```modelica
> connect(port_a, coldPlate.port_a)      -- Branch 1 GPU inlet
> connect(coldPlate.port_b, coldPlate2.port_a)  -- GPU outlet feeds CPU inlet
> connect(coldPlate2.port_b, port_b)     -- CPU outlet to blade exit
> connect(port_a, coldPlate1.port_a)     -- Branch 2 GPU inlet
> connect(coldPlate1.port_b, coldPlate3.port_a) -- GPU outlet feeds CPU inlet
> connect(coldPlate3.port_b, port_b)     -- CPU outlet to blade exit
> ```

### 2.4 Assumptions

- Default `nX=nY=nZ=1` gives a fully lumped chip — no spatial temperature gradients within the chip volume. Setting these >1 adds spatial resolution at the cost of simulation speed.
- Silicon material properties (ρ, cp, k) are fixed constants from the ORNL Silicon media model.
- Ambient blade air temperature is fixed at 25°C — the blade enclosure air is not dynamically modeled.
- `htc = 10 W/m²·K` is constant — no forced convection correlation inside the blade enclosure.
- Adiabatic boundary conditions at the inlet and outlet ends of the chip in Z direction are implied by the port connectivity pattern.

