## 5. CDU Model & Heat Exchanger Physics
**Files:** `Systems/Datacenter/Systems/CoolingBlock/Systems/CDU/Models/v0.mo`,
`Components/SubComponents/Fluid/HeatExchangers/CDU_HEX.mo`,
`Components/SubComponents/Fluid/HeatExchangers/BaseClasses/Simple_ITD_HX.mo`

The CDU thermally decouples the server coolant loop from the building hot water loop. It is the most control-critical component on the datacenter side.

### 5.1 Physical Configuration

```
Primary (building water):   port_a1 → dP_HTWS → valveCDU → HEX(primary) → dP_HTWR → port_b1
Secondary (server coolant): port_a2 → dP_CTW1 → HEX(secondary) → tee → CDUP pump → port_b2
                                                                         ↕
                                                                   press_CDU (expansion tank)
```

> **Source:** `CDU/Models/v0.mo` — all `connect()` statements in the equation section trace this flow path.

### 5.2 Nominal Operating Conditions

| Port | Temperature | Pressure | Flow Rate | Source Line |
|------|------------|----------|-----------|-------------|
| Server coolant inlet (port_a1) | 40°C | 64.7 psi | 15 kg/s | `CDU_T_a_start_1 = from_degC(40.0)`, `CDU_m_flow_start1 = 15.0` |
| Server coolant outlet (port_b1) | 30°C | 36.5 psi | — | `CDU_T_b_start_1 = from_degC(30.0)` |
| Building water inlet (port_a2) | 22.5°C | 79.7 psi | 12 kg/s | `CDU_T_a_start_2 = from_degC(22.5)`, `CDU_m_flow_start2 = 12.0` |
| Building water outlet (port_b2) | 35°C | 59.7 psi | — | `CDU_T_b_start_2 = from_degC(35.0)` |

> **Source:** `CDU_HEX.mo` — all parameter declarations in the initialization section.

### 5.3 Heat Transfer at Nominal Conditions (Derived)

```
Q_CDU = m_flow · cp · ΔT = 15 kg/s × 4184 J/kg·K × (40-30)°C ≈ 628 kW per CDU
Q_total = 628 kW × 25 CDUs ≈ 15.7 MW total server heat load
```

> **Source:** Derived from `CDU_HEX.mo` nominal parameters. Not explicitly stated in code — calculated from `CDU_m_flow_start1 = 15.0` and `CDU_T_a_start_1`/`CDU_T_b_start_1`.

### 5.4 Core Heat Exchanger Equation (Simple_ITD_HX)

```
Q_flow = UA · dT_ITD
dT_ITD = T_inlet_side1 - T_inlet_side2    (Inlet Temperature Difference)
```

> **Source:** `Simple_ITD_HX.mo` — explicit equation in the `equation` section:
> ```modelica
> Q_flow = UA*dT_ITD;
> dT_ITD = smooth(1, if abs(sensor_T_a1.T - sensor_T_a2.T) <= 1e-4
>              then 0
>              else (sensor_T_a1.T - sensor_T_a2.T))
>              "Inlet Temperature Difference";
> ```
> The `smooth()` function prevents numerical discontinuities when the inlet temperatures are nearly equal.

### 5.5 Discretization — 5 Nodes Per Side

The HX is split into `nV=5` nodes. Heat is distributed equally:

```
Q_node = Q_flow / nV    (at each node, Q_total / 5 is exchanged)
```

> **Source:** `CDU_HEX.mo`:
> ```modelica
> BaseClasses.Simple_ITD_HX CDU_HEX(
>     ...
>     nV=5,
>     counterCurrent=true, ...)
> ```
> And in `Simple_ITD_HX.mo`:
> ```modelica
> Modelica.Blocks.Sources.RealExpression boundary_1_input[nV](each y=-Q_flow/nV)
> Modelica.Blocks.Sources.RealExpression boundary_2_input[nV](each y=Q_flow/nV)
> ```

### 5.6 Counter-Current Configuration

The two fluid streams flow in opposite directions — maximizes heat transfer driving force:

```
Side 1 (hot):  port_a1 → → → → port_b1
Side 2 (cold): port_b2 ← ← ← ← port_a2
```

> **Source:** `CDU_HEX.mo`:
> ```modelica
> BaseClasses.Simple_ITD_HX CDU_HEX(
>     ...
>     counterCurrent=true, ...)
> ```

### 5.7 UA Lookup Table (Empirical)

The overall heat transfer coefficient-area product is NOT constant — it is interpolated from a 2D empirical table as a function of both fluid volumetric flow rates:

```
UA = UATable(Vol_flow_side1, Vol_flow_side2) × 1000 [W/kW conversion] × UA_corr_mod
```

UA values range from ~1.9 to ~73 kW/K over the operating range.

> **Source:** `CDU_HEX.mo`:
> ```modelica
> Modelica.Blocks.Tables.CombiTable2Ds UATable(table=[0, 0.001261804, ...; ...])
> Modelica.Blocks.Sources.RealExpression port_a1_volFlow(
>     y=port_a1.m_flow / CDU_HEX.Medium_1.density_ph(port_b1.p, port_a1.h_outflow))
> Modelica.Blocks.Sources.RealExpression port_a2_volFlow(
>     y=port_a2.m_flow / CDU_HEX.Medium_2.density_ph(port_b2.p, port_a2.h_outflow))
> ```
> Volumetric flow rates for each side are computed from mass flow rate / density, then used as table inputs.

### 5.8 UA Correction Factor

```modelica
UA=UATable.y*1e3*UA_corr_mod
```

```modelica
redeclare replaceable Data.v0 data(UA_corr_mod=1.2)
```

> **Source:** `CDU_HEX.mo` — `CDU_HEX` instantiation connects UATable output with `UA_corr_mod` multiplier. `CDU/Models/v0.mo` sets `UA_corr_mod=1.2` — a 20% upward correction calibrated against real Frontier hardware measurements.

### 5.9 Hydraulic Resistances

```modelica
parameter TRANSFORM.Units.HydraulicResistance R_Sec = 11000  "Sec. side hydraulic resistance"
parameter TRANSFORM.Units.HydraulicResistance R_Pri = 8000   "Pri. side hydraulic resistance"
```

> **Source:** `CDU_HEX.mo` — parameter declarations. Secondary (server) side has higher resistance (11,000) reflecting tighter microchannel geometry. Primary (building water) side has lower resistance (8,000) reflecting larger pipe diameters.

### 5.10 Fluid Volumes Inside HX

```modelica
constant Modelica.Units.SI.Volume V_1 = from_galUS(48.3-21.9)   -- side 1 fluid volume
constant Modelica.Units.SI.Volume V_2 = from_galUS(48.3-21.9)   -- side 2 fluid volume
```

> **Source:** `Simple_ITD_HX.mo` — constants declared at top of model. Both sides have equal fluid volume derived from gallon measurements (48.3 - 21.9 = 26.4 US gallons per side ≈ 0.1 m³).

### 5.11 CDU Control Signals

| Signal | Direction | Source Line |
|--------|-----------|-------------|
| opening_CDUOut | Input → valveCDU.opening | `connect(controlBus.opening_CDUOut, valveCDU.opening)` |
| CDUP_Nrel | Input → CDUP.inputSignal | `connect(controlBus.CDUP_Nrel, CDUP.inputSignal)` |
| T_CabSup | Output from CabSup_pT.T | `connect(controlBus.T_CabSup, CabSup_pT.T)` |
| T_CabRet | — | `connect(controlBus.p_CabRet, CabRet_pT.p)` |
| W_flow_CDUP | Summary output | `W_flow_CDUP=CDUP.W*CDUP.nParallel` |

> **Source:** `CDU/Models/v0.mo` — all `connect()` statements involving `controlBus`.

### 5.12 Key Safety Constraint

`T_CabSup` (server coolant supply temperature) is the primary safety constraint for ML optimization — servers overheat if this exceeds a threshold.

> **Source:** `CDU/Models/v0.mo`:
> ```modelica
> connect(controlBus.T_CabSup, CabSup_pT.T)
> ```
> `CabSup_pT` measures coolant temperature at `port_b2` — the outlet going to server cabinets.

